The Pitts Special S-1 aircrafts (S for “School,” one for “1”) were built in the late 1920s as a cost effective and low cost aerobatic/trainer airplane. They were not successful. When they went out of production in the 1930’s they were almost totally forgotten. That is until Charles “Chippy” Collins a WW2 fighter pilot got his hands on one and built it up to the state he left it in. After some modifications these airplanes became known as Collins Pits S-1. This restored version is being exhibited in Pitts Aircraft Museum’s static exhibit at AirVenture this year.
